---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ias/ias10.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        {
          id: "s1",
          label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage",
        },
        {
          id: "s2",
          label: "Scope & Applicability",
        },
        {
          id: "s3",
          label: "Adjusting vs Non-Adjusting",
        },
        {
          id: "s4",
          label: "Adjusting Events",
        },
        {
          id: "s5",
          label: "Non-Adjusting Events",
        },
        {
          id: "s6",
          label: "Going Concern",
        },
        {
          id: "s7",
          label: "Dividends",
        },
        {
          id: "s8",
          label: "Authorisation Date",
        },
        {
          id: "s9",
          label: "Disclosure Requirements",
        },
        {
          id: "s10",
          label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates",
        },
        {
          id: "s11",
          label: "IFRS vs US GAAP",
        },
        {
          id: "s12",
          label: "Statement Mapping",
        },
        {
          id: "s13",
          label: "Related Standards",
        },
        {
          id: "s14",
          label: "Glossary",
        },
        {
          id: "history",
          label: "History & Development",
        },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why the reporting date is not a hard information cut-off</div>
        <div class="principle">The Conceptual Framework requires financial statements to faithfully represent conditions existing AT the reporting date. IAS 10 recognises that evidence about those conditions sometimes only emerges AFTER year end but BEFORE the statements are finalised — and that this evidence should not be ignored simply because of timing.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IAS 10 MAPS TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Faithful representation</strong> — adjusting events update the numbers so year-end balances reflect conditions that genuinely existed at that date, using the best available evidence</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Relevance</strong> — material non-adjusting events, though not changing year-end balances, are disclosed because they inform users' decisions about the future</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Going concern assumption</strong> — a foundational assumption under IAS 1 that IAS 10 requires be reassessed using ALL information up to authorisation, not frozen at the reporting date</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IAS 10 essentially extends the "measurement window" for existing conditions from the reporting date to the authorisation date — a deliberate Framework-consistent trade-off between timeliness and faithful representation.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Measurement principles applied</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 10 does not introduce new measurement bases — it determines WHICH date's evidence should be used to apply the measurement bases already prescribed by other standards.</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Event type</span><span>Effect</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Adjusting event</span><span>Updates the MEASUREMENT of an existing recognised item, or triggers new recognition, using year-end conditions clarified by later evidence</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Non-adjusting event</span><span>No change to recognition/measurement; DISCLOSURE only, since the condition arose after the reporting date</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info" style="margin-bottom:0;">ℹ️ <strong style="font-weight:500">Qualitative characteristics in action:</strong> the adjusting/non-adjusting distinction is the clearest practical application of the Framework's line between <strong style="font-weight:500">correcting a measurement of what already existed</strong> versus <strong style="font-weight:500">informing users about a genuinely new development</strong>.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IAS 10 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 10 prescribes when an entity should adjust its financial statements for events after the reporting period, and the disclosures an entity should give about the date the financial statements were authorised for issue, and about events after the reporting period.</div>
        <div class="elabel">THE REPORTING WINDOW COVERED</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Events after the reporting period are those events, favourable and unfavourable, that occur between the END OF THE REPORTING PERIOD and the DATE THE FINANCIAL STATEMENTS ARE AUTHORISED FOR ISSUE — this window can span several weeks or months depending on an entity's reporting timetable.</div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top:9px;margin-bottom:0;">⚠️ Applies to EVERY entity with a reporting period, regardless of size or listing status — there are no scope exemptions.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Events after the reporting period</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">Those events, favourable and unfavourable, that occur between the end of the reporting period and the date the financial statements are authorised for issue.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Adjusting event</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">An event that provides evidence of conditions that EXISTED at the end of the reporting period.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Non-adjusting event</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">An event that is indicative of conditions that AROSE after the end of the reporting period.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Adjusting vs Non-Adjusting — The Core Test</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The single distinguishing question</div>
        <div class="principle">There is only one question that determines classification: DID THE CONDITION EXIST AT THE REPORTING DATE, with the later event simply providing more/better evidence of it? Or did the condition ARISE only after the reporting date?</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div class="spec-box" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <div class="spec-title" style="color:var(--color-text-success);">Adjusting event → recognise/remeasure</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.5;">Condition existed at year end; the subsequent event is EVIDENCE of that condition. Financial statements are adjusted to reflect it.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="spec-box" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <div class="spec-title" style="color:var(--color-text-success);">Non-adjusting event → disclose only</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.5;">Condition arose entirely AFTER year end. No new recognition or remeasurement is permitted; only disclosure if material.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;">⚠️ Once financial statements are AUTHORISED FOR ISSUE, an entity should NOT adjust for events occurring after that date, even if material — such events fall entirely outside the scope of the reported financial statements (though may require separate communication, e.g. a press release).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Adjusting Events — Examples &amp; Worked Example</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Classic examples of adjusting events</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Settlement of a court case AFTER year end that confirms the entity already had a present obligation at the reporting date</span>
          <span>Bankruptcy of a customer AFTER year end, confirming a trade receivable was already impaired at year end</span>
          <span>Sale of inventory AFTER year end providing evidence of its net realisable value at year end</span>
          <span>Determination AFTER year end of the cost of assets purchased, or proceeds from assets sold, before year end</span>
          <span>Determination AFTER year end of profit-sharing/bonus payments if the entity had a present legal or constructive obligation at year end</span>
          <span>Discovery of fraud or errors showing the financial statements were incorrect</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Worked example — customer bankruptcy</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Receivable written off after year end</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            A company's year end is 31 December 20X2. A major customer owing $85,000 (recognised in full as a receivable at year end, no allowance) files for bankruptcy on 15 February 20X3, before the financial statements are authorised for issue on 1 March 20X3. Investigation shows the customer's financial difficulties had been building since November 20X2.
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            This is an ADJUSTING event — the bankruptcy provides evidence the customer was ALREADY in financial difficulty at 31 December 20X2, meaning the receivable was already impaired at year end.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="entry"><span class="dr">Dr</span><div class="etxt">Impairment loss (bad debt expense) $85,000<div class="badges"><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></div></div></div>
        <div class="entry"><span class="cr">Cr</span><div class="etxt">Trade receivables (allowance) $85,000<div class="badges"><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></div></div></div>
        <div class="note">The 20X2 financial statements are adjusted BEFORE authorisation — this is not a subsequent-year 20X3 entry.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Non-Adjusting Events — Examples &amp; Worked Example</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Classic examples of non-adjusting events</div>
        <div class="cross-list">
          <span>A major business combination, or disposal of a major subsidiary, after year end</span>
          <span>Announcing a plan to discontinue an operation, major purchases/disposals of assets, or expropriation of major assets by government, after year end</span>
          <span>Destruction of a major production plant by a fire AFTER the reporting period</span>
          <span>Announcing/commencing a major restructuring AFTER year end</span>
          <span>Abnormally large changes AFTER the reporting period in asset prices or foreign exchange rates</span>
          <span>Changes in tax rates or tax law enacted/announced AFTER year end</span>
          <span>Entering into significant commitments or contingent liabilities, e.g. issuing guarantees, AFTER year end</span>
          <span>Commencing major litigation arising SOLELY out of events occurring after the reporting period</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Worked example — fire destroys a warehouse</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Warehouse fire after year end</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            A fire completely destroys one of the entity's warehouses, along with $2m of inventory, on 20 January 20X3 (year end 31 December 20X2), before authorisation for issue. The fire was caused by an electrical fault that occurred entirely after year end.
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            This is a NON-ADJUSTING event — the condition (the fire and resulting loss) arose entirely AFTER 31 December 20X2. The 20X2 financial statements are NOT adjusted; no write-down of the warehouse/inventory is recorded in 20X2.<br><br>
            <strong>Required action:</strong> disclose the nature of the event and an estimate of its financial effect (or a statement that such an estimate cannot be made) in the notes to the 20X2 financial statements, GIVEN materiality.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">If the loss is severe enough to bring the going concern basis into question (Section 6), it could instead require preparation of the financial statements on a wholly different basis — a much more significant consequence.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Going Concern — The Critical Override</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why going concern deterioration is always adjusting — even from post-year-end causes</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 10 contains a unique rule: an entity must NOT prepare its financial statements on a going concern basis if management determines, AFTER the reporting period, either that it intends to liquidate the entity or cease trading, or that it has no realistic alternative but to do so.</div>
        <div class="callout danger" style="margin-bottom:0;">✕ <strong style="font-weight:600">This is treated as an adjusting event of the most fundamental kind</strong> — NOT because the underlying cause existed at year end (it may not have), but because the deterioration in going concern status is so fundamental that the ENTIRE BASIS of preparation changes, requiring a switch to break-up/liquidation basis accounting for the whole set of financial statements, regardless of whether the specific cause was adjusting or non-adjusting in the conventional sense.</div>
        <div class="note">This is a deliberately distinct rule from the general adjusting/non-adjusting framework and is one of the most heavily tested conceptual points in IAS 10 — going concern deterioration after year end but before authorisation ALWAYS requires action, unlike ordinary non-adjusting events which are merely disclosed.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Dividends Declared After the Reporting Period</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Dividends are always non-adjusting</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;margin-bottom:9px;">If dividends to holders of equity instruments are declared AFTER the reporting period, an entity should NOT recognise those dividends as a liability at the end of the reporting period — there is no present obligation at year end, since the declaration (the obligating event) has not yet occurred.</div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-bottom:0;">⚠️ This applies EVEN IF the dividend relates to profits earned during the reporting period being reported on — the KEY event is the declaration date, not the period the profit relates to.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Worked example &amp; disclosure requirement</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 3: Dividend declared after year end</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            A company's board declares a final dividend of $0.30 per share on 10 February 20X3, in respect of the year ended 31 December 20X2, at a board meeting before the financial statements were authorised for issue.
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>No liability</strong> is recognised in the 20X2 statement of financial position — the obligation did not exist at 31 December 20X2.<br>
            <strong>Disclosure only</strong>: the amount is disclosed either on the face of the SOFP as a separate component of equity, or in the notes, per IAS 1/IAS 10.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">A very commonly tested scenario — students often incorrectly recognise the dividend as a year-end liability simply because it "relates to" the reported profits.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Authorisation Date — Determining the Window's End</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Identifying the "date of authorisation for issue"</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;margin-bottom:9px;">The process for authorising financial statements for issue varies depending on management structure, statutory requirements, and procedures for preparing/finalising them — the ENTITY must determine, based on its specific facts, when this date occurs, and disclose it.</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SCENARIO A — MANAGEMENT ISSUES TO A SUPERVISORY BOARD</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">The financial statements are authorised for issue when management authorises them for issue to the supervisory board, NOT when the supervisory board subsequently approves them.</div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SCENARIO B — SHAREHOLDER APPROVAL REQUIRED</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">The date of authorisation for issue is the date the financial statements are issued (e.g. to shareholders for approval), NOT the later date shareholders approve them at the AGM — shareholder approval does not extend the subsequent events window.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Required disclosures</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">MANDATORY DISCLOSURES</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span>Date the financial statements were authorised for issue, and WHO gave that authorisation</span>
              <span>If the entity's owners or others have the power to amend the financial statements after issue, that fact must be disclosed</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">FOR EACH MATERIAL CATEGORY OF NON-ADJUSTING EVENT</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span>The nature of the event</span>
              <span>An estimate of its financial effect, or a statement that such an estimate cannot be made</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">If new information is received AFTER year end about conditions that existed at year end, an entity updates its disclosures about those conditions in light of the new information, even where the underlying event does not require a formal adjustment.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Whether an event provides evidence of conditions existing at year end (adjusting) or reflects entirely new conditions (non-adjusting) — the single most tested judgement in the standard</span>
              <span>Determining the correct authorisation-for-issue date, especially in multi-tier governance structures</span>
              <span>Whether a going concern deterioration is severe enough to require a change in the basis of preparation</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>The financial effect of a material non-adjusting event, where an estimate is practicable</span>
              <span>Quantifying an adjustment where new evidence only partially clarifies a year-end condition</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;">⚠️ A single event can sometimes contain BOTH adjusting and non-adjusting elements — e.g. a court case settlement might confirm an existing obligation (adjusting) while ALSO triggering a new, separate claim arising only from the settlement itself (non-adjusting) — careful separation is required.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IFRS vs US GAAP — Key Differences</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — IAS 10 vs US GAAP (ASC 855)</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IAS 10)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Terminology</span><span>Adjusting / non-adjusting events</span><span>"Recognized" and "non-recognized" subsequent events — conceptually equivalent</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>End of the evaluation window</span><span>Date financial statements are authorised for issue</span><span>SEC registrants: date financial statements are ISSUED (filed); other entities: date available to be issued — subtly different from "authorised"</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Re-evaluation on reissuance</span><span>Generally not re-evaluated for a NEW subsequent events window on reissuance</span><span>SEC registrants generally do NOT re-evaluate; other entities may need to evaluate through the reissuance date under specific circumstances</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Going concern deterioration</span><span>Fundamental override — changes the basis of preparation entirely</span><span>Similar substantive requirement (ASC 205-40) to evaluate and disclose substantial doubt about going concern</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">The two frameworks are broadly converged in substance; the main practical nuance is the precise DATE used to define the end of the subsequent-events window (authorisation vs issuance), which can matter in specific fact patterns for SEC filers.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping — Where Every Subsequent Event Lands</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Adjusting event — remeasurement of an existing asset/liability</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Recognised in the reporting period being finalised, not the following period</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Adjusting event — new obligation confirmed to have existed at year end</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>New provision/liability recognised in the current reporting period</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Non-adjusting event, material</span><span>—</span><span>No SOFP/SOPL change; disclosed in notes only</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Going concern deterioration after year end</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Entire basis of preparation changes to break-up/liquidation basis</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Dividends declared after year end</span><span>—</span><span>NOT recognised as a liability; disclosed only</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Discovery of fraud/error affecting reported figures</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Adjusting event; corrected before authorisation, distinct from IAS 8 prior period error correction after issuance</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Authorisation date and authorising body</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Mandatory disclosure in every set of financial statements</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Estimated financial effect of material non-adjusting events</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Or a statement that no estimate can be made</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards — Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">How IAS 10 connects across the IFRS body of literature</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 1</span><span>Requires management's going concern assessment to consider ALL available information, including events after the reporting period — the direct trigger for IAS 10's going concern override</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 37</span><span>Court case settlements and confirmed obligations arising as adjusting events determine whether a provision recognised (or not recognised) at year end under IAS 37 needs to change</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 2</span><span>Post-year-end sale prices provide adjusting evidence for testing inventory's net realisable value at the reporting date</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 36</span><span>Post-year-end evidence (e.g. a confirmed customer loss, market crash) may indicate impairment indicators existed at year end, triggering an adjusting remeasurement</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 8</span><span>Distinguishes an IAS 10 adjusting event (corrected BEFORE authorisation) from an IAS 8 prior period error (discovered AFTER a prior period's statements were already authorised/issued)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 33</span><span>Certain post-year-end share transactions (e.g. bonus issues, share splits) require RETROSPECTIVE adjustment of EPS calculations even though they are otherwise non-adjusting events</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 5</span><span>A decision to sell a major asset/operation made after year end is generally a non-adjusting event, since the "held for sale" criteria are assessed as at year end, not a later date</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="three-col">
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Reporting period</div><div class="glossary-def">The period covered by the financial statements — typically 12 months, ending on the reporting date.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Authorisation for issue</div><div class="glossary-def">The point at which the entity's management/board formally releases the financial statements for external use — defines the end of the subsequent events window.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Break-up basis</div><div class="glossary-def">A basis of accounting used when an entity is no longer a going concern — assets and liabilities are measured at amounts expected to be realised on liquidation, rather than on an ongoing operational basis.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Obligating event</div><div class="glossary-def">An event that creates a legal or constructive obligation, leaving the entity no realistic alternative but to settle it — the trigger for dividend recognition under IAS 10/IAS 37.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Material</div><div class="glossary-def">Information whose omission or misstatement could reasonably be expected to influence users' decisions — the threshold for requiring non-adjusting event disclosure.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Supervisory board</div><div class="glossary-def">A separate governance body (common in some jurisdictions) that may need to approve financial statements AFTER management has already authorised them for issue to that board.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="legend">
      <div class="ltitle">Financial statement key</div>
      <div class="lrow">
        <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> Statement of financial position (balance sheet)
        <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> Statement of profit or loss (income statement)
        <span class="badge bo">OCI</span> Other comprehensive income
        <span class="badge be">Equity</span> Statement of changes in equity
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
